      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "ADAM33 is the candidate gene most commonly associated with asthma and airway hyperreactivity (AHR)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to determine whether level of AHR is associated with certain alleles or haplotypes of the ADAM33 gene in asthmatic children."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "One hundred and nine asthmatic children and 46 controls from the general population were examined with spirometry before and after histamine and methacholine inhalation. All subjects were genotyped for single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) of the ADAM33 gene. Haplotypes were determined according to genotypes of the patient's parents."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "We found the three most frequent ADAM33 haplotypes (a1-3) were associated with the highest level of AHR to methacholine and histamine in 66% of asthmatic children. The paternally transmitted GGGCTTTCGCA haplotype was seen in 73.3% asthmatic children with serious AHR to methacholine challenge (paternal and maternal origin of haplotype 73.3% to 37.5, P=0.046) Significant differences in the relative frequency of paternal haplotypes with high levels of AHR to histamine were found (P=0.013)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "ADAM33 haplotypes (a1, a2, a3) are associated with severity of AHR and are significantly more often transmitted in the paternal line."
        }
      ]},
